WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
One common modern leak pattern is unintended retention.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:10.600
A shared ownership cycle can keep an object graph alive even after the rest of the program no longer needs it.

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:15.340
Each object remains alive because another object in the cycle still owns it.

4
00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:18.980
Parent-child relationships need a clear ownership direction.

5
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:25.640
A parent may own children, while children observe the parent through a weak reference or another non-owning mechanism.

6
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.820
If both directions use shared ownership, cleanup can become dependent on a cycle breaking by chance.

7
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.660
Callback captures also deserve review.

8
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.400
Capturing a shared pointer may be the right choice when queued work must keep a target alive.

9
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:46.300
It may also retain an object after a screen closes, a request ends, or a service begins shutdown.

10
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.200
The code should make that lifetime extension intentional.

11
00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:56.440
Caches, observer lists, event systems, task queues, and registries need lifecycle rules.

12
00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:03.740
A cache should define size limits, eviction policy, and whether entries own objects or merely observe them.

13
00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.000
A callback list should define registration and deregistration behavior.

14
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:14.120
std::weak_ptr can break cycles safely when the observed object is managed by shared ownership.

15
00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:19.900
Code can lock the weak pointer for temporary access and handle the case where the object no longer exists.

16
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.880
That failure path is part of the design, not an exceptional surprise.

17
00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.360
Retention paths should be reviewed explicitly.

18
00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:37.660
Ask what can keep this object alive, how long those owners can last, whether any relationship is cyclic, and what cleanup or expiration path releases the object.

19
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.760
If a resource can stay retained forever, the design needs a boundary.

20
00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:46.040
When reviewing retained state, include indirect owners.

21
00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:54.700
Scheduled work, promises, lambdas, GUI bindings, background tasks, and telemetry buffers may all hold references.

22
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.280
The retention decision should be intentional, bounded, and visible.
